@@s001 Code: Gale-Shapley in Java
@@s002 Code: Gale-Shapley in Java
@@s003 The proposal algorithm in Java: preferences as arrays, rank of the receivers, queue of the free proposers.
@@s004 Preferences and rank
@@s005 Each proposer has a list of preferences. Each receiver, instead, needs a fast question: <i>which do I prefer between two?</i> That is why I build the rank.
@@s006 Preferences and rank
@@s007 List of preferences of the proposers: <code>pref[p][k]</code> is the k-th receiver preferred by <code>p</code>.
@@s008 The rank inverts the list: now <code>rango[r][p]</code> gives the position of <code>p</code>, and the comparison is a single access.
@@s009 Syntax notes
@@s010 <code>StringTokenizer</code> reads the integers of a line; <code>trim()</code> removes the spaces at the edges of the line.
@@s011 The proposal loop
@@s012 While there is a free proposer, that proposer proposes to the next receiver on the list. The receiver decides whether to keep them.
@@s013 The proposal loop
@@s014 <code>prossimo[p]++</code> uses the current value and then increments: the next proposal of <code>p</code> will be the following one.
@@s015 The receiver compares the rank: a smaller number is more liked.
@@s016 The partner left behind goes back into the queue of the free ones.
@@s017 Syntax notes
@@s018 <code>partnerDi[r] == -1</code>: the value <code>-1</code> means "free", because the real indices go from 0 to n−1.
